    <h2>The pros &amp; cons &mdash; honestly framed</h2>

    <h3>The rewards</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Timeless wealth preservation.</strong> Freehold title on Bassein Road doesn&rsquo;t decay after year 30 like leasehold. Value holds across generations.</li>
      <li><strong>Pre-emptive capital benchmarking.</strong> The 5 upcoming GLS sites nearby act as a capital shield &mdash; developers bidding on those plots will pay increasingly higher land prices, meaning future launches in the same precinct will list at higher PSF than what you pay for The Serra today.</li>
      <li><strong>Superior cashflow generation.</strong> A 3.5% projected gross yield in the CCR is extremely rare for a freehold asset. Proximity to Mount Elizabeth Novena, Tan Tock Seng Hospital and the NTU medical school ensures a continuous influx of high-budget professional tenants.</li>
      <li><strong>Space you actually paid for.</strong> First freehold GFA-harmonised CCR launch means you&rsquo;re not paying PSF on aircon ledges and phantom voids &mdash; a structural pricing-efficiency advantage over the older freehold stock in the same district.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The risks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Boutique size &amp; resale liquidity (133 units).</strong> A small unit count is a double-edged sword. It guarantees exclusivity, quiet facilities and low internal competition when renting, but resale transaction volume will be lower &mdash; which can occasionally make bank valuations on a steep upward move slower to catch up. Expect average, not exceptional, resale liquidity.</li>
      <li><strong>Proximity, not integration, with the growth hotspot.</strong> Mount Pleasant is a government-recognised growth zone, but The Serra is 1.16&nbsp;km from the boundary. You benefit from the macro infrastructure spillover, but you&rsquo;re not sitting inside the maximum-uplift zone.</li>
    </ul>

